Z||000000||rockcrawl||01-01-2001||04:37 PM||||I've been thinking a lot about doing some ametuer autocross racing. Where can I find more info? I may not be ready to get started in the sport, but I'd like to watch a few races. Is there anywhere in or near eastern PA where autocross racing takes place?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||01-01-2001||05:38 PM||||might be nice to know what the simplest mods you can do are that will still keep you in the most amature class. hate to do some apperance or wheel type mod and then find out it puts you in with the pro class or something like that.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||01-01-2001||07:18 PM||||There is the Stock class, which means you cant really add anything(you also need to use stock rim sizes I believe)!<P>So all you people who are putting off autocrossing cause you need to do "mods" your wasting your time because you just go up in class.<P>The major factor in this class  is TIRES!<BR>the serious ones use the DOT legal racing tires<BR>Kumho victoracer or Hoosier A3S03...  BFG is discontinuing their tire, and the Yoko's arent very popular out by me.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>Bring it to the track.<BR>Lets see if all those mods really helped!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||01-01-2001||07:31 PM||||I have run a few events in harrisburg as well as Rome NY. they hold the divisionals in that area every year. doesn't matter what you have done or how fancy your tires are , seat time is the MAJOR key , then tires ..then the rest. I have seen good drives blow the doors off of crap drivers in hot cars (ie vipers , porsche's) ect. guys come out and stab the pedal like a murderer killing his victim , thinking there flying. There is an artice somewhere on the net about a guy who brought out a real Lambo diablo , and got beat by a metro GSi , on an open course..<P> perfect Practice makes perfect<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||01-01-2001||08:49 PM||||so i can jump in my 4cyl fiero and do alright (myself being the weak link)? what if i've already changed tire size (16's) and lowered it (upcoming)? if i get to change engines (not for auto x) would i have to change classes? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||01-01-2001||08:55 PM||||I have discovered the SCCA website at <A HREF="http://www.scca.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org</A>  , and the classification listings for Solo II (autocross) at <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/index.html</A> which tells you what you can and cannot do for each class.<P>Very interesting stuff! I'll definately have to get involved in the future, it sounds like a lot of fun!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark||01-01-2001||11:19 PM||||Jonathan's right about seat time being the major key.  <P>At a local SCCA event, I watched a guy in a street prepared '79 BMW 320i ( a car dear to me, but not well regarded for its handling or power) out drive some pretty impressive competition, like a BMW M3, Honda S2000, etc.  I asked him what on earth he did to make that car run so well (I owned one for 10 years, and mine surely didn't run like that!)  He did admit to some suspension tuning, and he was running Hoosiers (on 13" wheels), but his answer was "I've been driving this car a long time."  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||01-02-2001||01:32 PM||||Not disagreeing with the seat time factor being important(which seems pretty obvious to me), but the question was about mods putting one into a higher class.  There is no class based upon seat time, except maybe for the rookie of the year title which is not really a class.<P>Even if you have 30 years of racing experience, in C-Stock (which Fiero V6's are in) A reasonably competant autocrosser with DOT legal racing tires will beat a seasoned veteran with normal street tires in the same class.  It is Basic physics...  If one car can pull 1.0-1.1 lateral g's while the other car can only pull .85-.90 g's there is good likelyhood that one run of the competant driver will will be good enough so that they exceeded the limitations  of the veterans car. so there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>There might be an STS category by you folks, but PAX doesnt seem that accurate to me.<P>BTW Upgrading motors ALWAYS puts you up in class...  that is unless you are putting in a rebuilt motor in which case you need to make sure you are not too overbored (like anyone is going to check) But there are alot of restrictions for motor mods to stay in the stock class.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
